<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Myriokephalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1176">1176</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Myriocephalum · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-seljuk-wars">Byzantine–Seljuk wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Myriokephalon was fought on 17 September 1176 in the mountains of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egirdir">Eğirdir</a>. The Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> ambushed the army of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> in a narrow pass as it marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Manuel escaped and agreed a truce, but the defeat ended Byzantine hopes of recovering central Anatolia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-myriokephalon.jpg" alt="Battle of Myriokephalon" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crusades_surprised_by_turks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>A series of Byzantine campaigns between 1158 and 1161 ended in a treaty in which the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> accepted a form of subordination to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, and he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The long peace let Manuel concentrate on the west and the east, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and reducing the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status, but it also let Kilij Arslan eliminate his internal rivals. After the death of Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1174 he destroyed the Danishmend emirates and drove out his brother Shahinshah, Manuel's vassal. The peace collapsed in 1175 when he refused to hand over the Danishmend lands as the treaty required.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Manuel gathered a very large army at Lopadion and rejected a new offer of peace from Kilij Arslan. A force under Andronikos Vatatzes sent towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasia</a> was destroyed in an ambush. The Turks destroyed crops and poisoned water supplies, and harassed the main army towards the Tzivritze Pass, near the ruined fortress of Myriokephalon. Manuel decided to force the pass despite the danger, perhaps because the army lacked forage and water and dysentery had broken out. Modern estimates of its size run from 25,000 to 35,000 men.</p><p>The vanguard and main division passed with few casualties, but the following divisions did not secure the heights or keep close order. The Turks closed the trap and fell especially on the right wing, whose commander Baldwin was killed, and then on the baggage and siege trains. A dust storm added to the confusion. Manuel is said to have sat down and awaited his fate before his officers roused him. The army fought free of the pass and camped with the divisions that had gone ahead, repelling attacks through the night.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Both sides had casualties, but Manuel's siege equipment was lost and he could not attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Kilij Arslan sent an envoy to negotiate; under the truce Manuel was to demolish his forts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> and Sublaeum, and he destroyed only Sublaeum's. Manuel compared the defeat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in a message to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> won a victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Meander</a> the following year, and Manuel made a peace in 1179. Myriokephalon nevertheless proved that the empire could not destroy Seljuk power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and it marked the end of Byzantine attempts to recover the plateau.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Myriokephalon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1176 Kilij Arslan II trapped and defeated the army of the Byzantine emperor Manuel I Komnenos at the <b>Battle of Myriokephalon</b>, ending Byzantine hopes of recovering central Anatolia; two years later he absorbed the rival Danishmendid emirate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li></ul></section>
